1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,064
Hey, everyone.

2
00:00:01,152 --> 00:00:02,255
My name is Harini.

3
00:00:02,848 --> 00:00:07,416
I am an engineering leader at one
of the big fintech companies, and

4
00:00:07,449 --> 00:00:09,268
I manage their messaging infra.

5
00:00:10,126 --> 00:00:14,852
In today's session, I'll talk about
running Apache Kafka for mission-critical

6
00:00:14,914 --> 00:00:20,713
Tier Zero workloads, how to design it
to survive losing an entire region, and

7
00:00:20,713 --> 00:00:25,558
how to prove that it works as expected
under such disastrous scenarios by

8
00:00:25,572 --> 00:00:30,999
breaking it on purpose, because we don't
want any surprises at the wrong time

9
00:00:31,998 --> 00:00:33,594
So let's define the goal.

10
00:00:34,045 --> 00:00:35,307
What's our goal here?

11
00:00:36,094 --> 00:00:40,598
Our goal is to keep the Kafka cluster
operational and continue serving

12
00:00:40,618 --> 00:00:45,478
traffic as normally as possible,
even if an entire region goes down.

13
00:00:46,487 --> 00:00:50,104
As you can see in the animation,
when region C goes down,

14
00:00:50,253 --> 00:00:53,514
traffic still continues to flow
through the surviving regions.

15
00:00:54,499 --> 00:00:59,029
We can even continue rolling the
brokers one at a time because

16
00:00:59,286 --> 00:01:03,357
break glass maintenance cannot
always wait for an outage to end.

17
00:01:03,992 --> 00:01:07,634
Let's say if we want to apply
an emergency patch to handle the

18
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:09,943
outage, we should be able to do it.

19
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:15,136
But defining for this level of resilience
raises three important questions.

20
00:01:15,903 --> 00:01:18,571
What survives when an
entire region goes down?

21
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:24,287
How do we prove the system behaves as,
as expected when it actually goes down?

22
00:01:24,851 --> 00:01:26,753
And how do we safely recover?

23
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,184
These three questions will guide
us through the rest of the talk.

24
00:01:32,854 --> 00:01:36,452
First, let's look at how
the Kafka cluster is set up.

25
00:01:37,427 --> 00:01:43,794
In our design, we have three independent
isolated regions with two broker in each.

26
00:01:44,833 --> 00:01:51,301
Each broker is assigned a rack value
corresponding to its regions A, B, or C.

27
00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:55,540
This is important because it
allows us to distribute the

28
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:57,631
replicas across failure domains.

29
00:01:58,108 --> 00:02:01,451
In our scenario, each
failure domain is a region.

30
00:02:02,685 --> 00:02:06,390
With replication factor of
six, each region holds two

31
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:08,171
replicas of every partition.

32
00:02:08,976 --> 00:02:13,947
So if region C goes down, we
still have four surviving replicas

33
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:15,986
spread across regions A and B.

34
00:02:16,957 --> 00:02:22,101
But having four surviving replicas is
only useful if the topic and the client

35
00:02:22,123 --> 00:02:27,647
configs allow Kafka to continue accepting
reads and writes with four replicas.

36
00:02:28,642 --> 00:02:32,804
This brings us to the next design
decision of defining when Kafka

37
00:02:32,813 --> 00:02:36,887
should continue accepting writes
and when it should stop to protect

38
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:43,571
durability Here we have a replication
factor of six, but we have configured

39
00:02:43,645 --> 00:02:45,494
minimum sync replicas to be three.

40
00:02:46,484 --> 00:02:48,292
This is an important design decision.

41
00:02:48,823 --> 00:02:54,432
When an entire region goes down, we still
have four surviving replicas, allowing

42
00:02:54,446 --> 00:02:56,477
Kafka to continue accepting writes.

43
00:02:57,351 --> 00:03:00,131
Now, let's say we need to
perform maintenance while that

44
00:03:00,132 --> 00:03:01,542
region is still unavailable.

45
00:03:01,883 --> 00:03:03,615
Let's say we need to apply some patch.

46
00:03:04,337 --> 00:03:08,998
As we roll the surviving brokers
one at a time, the ISR will

47
00:03:09,053 --> 00:03:11,149
temporarily drop from three to…

48
00:03:11,892 --> 00:03:16,222
from four to three as each broker
goes down and comes back up.

49
00:03:17,039 --> 00:03:22,590
Since we still meet the minimum ISR
requirement, the writes continue to work.

50
00:03:23,423 --> 00:03:28,584
That's the reason why we chose replication
factor of, of six, but set the min ISR

51
00:03:28,614 --> 00:03:33,507
to be three, because this gives us the
flexibility to do a rolling restart of

52
00:03:33,543 --> 00:03:35,961
the cluster even if one region is down.

53
00:03:36,949 --> 00:03:39,534
But there is a limit to
this availability guarantee.

54
00:03:40,166 --> 00:03:44,942
If we lose an entire region and
two additional brokers, then we

55
00:03:44,942 --> 00:03:46,779
are left with only two replicas.

56
00:03:47,827 --> 00:03:52,897
That falls below our configured minimum
ISR of three, so Kafka will start

57
00:03:53,009 --> 00:03:58,276
rejecting writes over compromising on
durability, and that's intentional.

58
00:03:58,925 --> 00:04:02,675
We are designing to tolerate
a regional outage plus one

59
00:04:02,721 --> 00:04:06,784
additional bro-broker failure or
restarts, not unlimited failures.

60
00:04:07,873 --> 00:04:09,341
But this is not enough still.

61
00:04:09,833 --> 00:04:13,767
We need to have some enforcement
on the client side as well to

62
00:04:13,769 --> 00:04:16,141
make sure it all works in place.

63
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:21,303
On the client side, all
producers writing tier zero data

64
00:04:21,391 --> 00:04:23,219
should use acks equal to all.

65
00:04:23,897 --> 00:04:25,029
What does that even mean?

66
00:04:25,786 --> 00:04:30,070
When a producer sends a write to
the partition leader, the data gets

67
00:04:30,070 --> 00:04:34,287
replicated to all other in-sync
replicas across all the regions.

68
00:04:35,118 --> 00:04:39,491
Only after the data has been successfully
replicated, the producer receives an

69
00:04:39,519 --> 00:04:41,536
acknowledgement back from the server.

70
00:04:42,543 --> 00:04:45,673
This is important for maintaining
our durability guarantees.

71
00:04:46,301 --> 00:04:50,922
So when one region goes down, we are
left with four surviving replicas.

72
00:04:51,622 --> 00:04:55,998
So the producer receives an ack back
when the data has been replicated

73
00:04:56,022 --> 00:04:57,686
to all of those four replicas.

74
00:04:57,774 --> 00:05:01,489
It doesn't wait for an ack
for all six to be done.

75
00:05:02,483 --> 00:05:06,635
As long as there are at least three
in-sync replicas, all of them have

76
00:05:06,635 --> 00:05:10,885
received the data, the producer can
receive the acknowledgement from Kafka

77
00:05:11,885 --> 00:05:16,717
So as you see, we have three
different layers here, and each one

78
00:05:16,784 --> 00:05:18,887
is only useful with the other three.

79
00:05:19,741 --> 00:05:23,685
If we remove any one layer or
we break any one layer, all of

80
00:05:23,731 --> 00:05:25,640
the Tier Zero guarantees break.

81
00:05:26,462 --> 00:05:30,081
We need all three of these
to work in tandem for our

82
00:05:30,087 --> 00:05:32,699
mission-critical Tier Zero workloads

83
00:05:33,699 --> 00:05:38,052
Now that we have discussed the brokers
and topic configurations, there's

84
00:05:38,109 --> 00:05:41,816
another layer we need to consider,
which is the controller quorum.

85
00:05:42,712 --> 00:05:44,017
Kafka 3.9

86
00:05:44,061 --> 00:05:48,203
and above supports dynamic
quorums, whereas older versions

87
00:05:48,383 --> 00:05:50,259
only support static quorums.

88
00:05:50,854 --> 00:05:54,388
So we need to approach this
differently depending on the versions.

89
00:05:55,045 --> 00:05:58,848
With static quorum, to achieve
our availability goal, we need

90
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:00,959
three controllers in each region.

91
00:06:01,898 --> 00:06:04,224
That gives us nine controllers in total.

92
00:06:04,489 --> 00:06:08,851
If one region goes down, we would
still have six out of nine controllers

93
00:06:08,894 --> 00:06:14,158
available, allowing us to roll them one
at a time without losing the majority.

94
00:06:14,439 --> 00:06:21,114
Because in that case, the majority
will be five out of nine, rolling one

95
00:06:21,116 --> 00:06:25,138
controller at a time when one region
is down would satisfy that guarantee.

96
00:06:26,361 --> 00:06:28,582
If you're running on Kafka 3.9

97
00:06:28,582 --> 00:06:33,007
or above and have configured the
cluster to use dynamic quorum, we

98
00:06:33,009 --> 00:06:38,554
can achieve the same with just five
controllers, two each in two regions

99
00:06:38,766 --> 00:06:40,630
and just one in the third region.

100
00:06:40,639 --> 00:06:44,777
We don't need nine controllers when
we have dynamic quorum enabled.

101
00:06:45,466 --> 00:06:48,622
So let's say when a region goes
down, that would leave us with

102
00:06:48,811 --> 00:06:50,318
three available controllers.

103
00:06:51,864 --> 00:06:53,962
Note that there is a
manual step involved here.

104
00:06:54,202 --> 00:06:59,347
We'll have to go and manually remove
the two unavailable controllers from the

105
00:06:59,367 --> 00:07:04,735
voters list, which would bring down the
total number of voters from five to three.

106
00:07:05,692 --> 00:07:08,324
Now, to reach majority, we just need two.

107
00:07:09,318 --> 00:07:14,246
That gives us the flexibility to still
roll the controllers one at a time

108
00:07:14,597 --> 00:07:19,570
when one region is down, because we
can still have a majority of two with

109
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:21,774
three controllers rolling one at a time

110
00:07:22,774 --> 00:07:27,238
Now that we have covered the architecture
and the cluster configurations, let's

111
00:07:27,239 --> 00:07:29,369
move on to the actual tests themselves.

112
00:07:29,982 --> 00:07:34,003
Here we have identified six
different failure scenarios that

113
00:07:34,070 --> 00:07:35,784
I'll walk through one by one.

114
00:07:36,784 --> 00:07:40,719
Actually, before we get into the
test details, let's look at how we

115
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:42,939
can simulate some of these failures.

116
00:07:43,941 --> 00:07:47,035
Simulating a region failure
is pretty straightforward.

117
00:07:47,534 --> 00:07:50,541
We can simply shut down all
the nodes in that region.

118
00:07:51,557 --> 00:07:56,141
To simulate regional isolation, where
the brokers are still running, but the

119
00:07:56,144 --> 00:08:01,279
regions can't communicate with each other,
we can just introduce firewall rules

120
00:08:01,339 --> 00:08:03,761
that blocks traffics between regions

121
00:08:04,761 --> 00:08:10,147
For WAN degradation, we can intentionally
introduce network latency and packet loss

122
00:08:10,219 --> 00:08:15,557
between regions to see how the cluster
behaves under degraded network conditions.

123
00:08:16,417 --> 00:08:21,586
And finally, to test the cluster under
load, we can use load generating clients

124
00:08:21,869 --> 00:08:24,194
on both the produce and consumer sides.

125
00:08:25,174 --> 00:08:29,819
These clients should have ways to ramp
traffic up or down in a controlled

126
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:32,352
manner while running these failure tests

127
00:08:33,352 --> 00:08:35,705
The first test is the most basic one.

128
00:08:36,388 --> 00:08:39,304
What happens when an
entire region goes down?

129
00:08:40,286 --> 00:08:44,873
We can simulate this by shutting down
all brokers running in one region,

130
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:47,821
let's say region C, for example.

131
00:08:48,609 --> 00:08:53,527
But remember, our goal is not just
to keep the cluster operational

132
00:08:53,585 --> 00:08:55,116
when one region goes down.

133
00:08:55,676 --> 00:08:59,845
We also want to be able to roll the
surviving brokers one at a time.

134
00:09:00,324 --> 00:09:02,520
So we'll test that scenario as well.

135
00:09:03,386 --> 00:09:06,906
Throughout the test, the cluster
should continue serving traffic.

136
00:09:07,252 --> 00:09:11,006
The producer should be able to
write and receive acknowledgments.

137
00:09:11,728 --> 00:09:17,748
The in-sync replicas count should remain
at three or more all the time, and no

138
00:09:17,815 --> 00:09:19,844
acknowledged writes should be lost.

139
00:09:20,874 --> 00:09:25,015
We can build a simple test suite
that tracks all successfully

140
00:09:25,058 --> 00:09:29,434
acknowledged writes during the test
and verifies that every one of those

141
00:09:29,438 --> 00:09:31,475
records can be read back from Kafka.

142
00:09:32,663 --> 00:09:36,348
While the test is running, we
should monitor producer error rates.

143
00:09:37,139 --> 00:09:40,891
As partition leaders move to
surviving brokers, clients

144
00:09:40,891 --> 00:09:44,077
may temporarily disconnect and
reconnect to the new leaders.

145
00:09:44,513 --> 00:09:49,057
So we might see a brief spike at
the beginning of the test in client

146
00:09:49,087 --> 00:09:53,333
errors, but things should stabilize
when the transition is complete.

147
00:09:53,797 --> 00:09:59,419
So this wraps up the first test, proving
that Kafka works when one region is

148
00:09:59,465 --> 00:10:05,732
down with the ability to still roll
the brokers one at a time if needed.

149
00:10:06,732 --> 00:10:08,049
Let's go to the second test.

150
00:10:08,570 --> 00:10:11,208
The second test is about
regional isolation.

151
00:10:11,748 --> 00:10:17,883
So here we see that all three regions are
still up and running, but we are going

152
00:10:17,891 --> 00:10:23,732
to simulate a scenario where region C is
completely isolated from regions A and B.

153
00:10:24,889 --> 00:10:29,413
So when you cut that connection or
when you add those firewall rules

154
00:10:29,436 --> 00:10:35,357
to block any communication between
regions A and B and C, you would see

155
00:10:35,358 --> 00:10:40,784
that the clients connected to regions
A and B, would still continue to work.

156
00:10:41,949 --> 00:10:46,076
We may see that there would be some
leader elections, and as a result

157
00:10:46,079 --> 00:10:50,287
of that, some clients would get
disconnected and reelected, reconnected,

158
00:10:50,645 --> 00:10:54,303
but then they should still continue
to work because of the surviving

159
00:10:54,311 --> 00:10:56,376
replicas in region f- A and B.

160
00:10:57,705 --> 00:11:01,716
so once the cluster stabilizes,
everything continues to work as

161
00:11:01,716 --> 00:11:03,686
normal, and there's no issues.

162
00:11:04,344 --> 00:11:06,408
But there's one important exception here.

163
00:11:06,962 --> 00:11:11,865
If a client is running in region C and
can only communicate with brokers in

164
00:11:11,865 --> 00:11:17,876
that region, it won't be successfully
able to read or write because region

165
00:11:17,879 --> 00:11:22,613
C no longer has enough in-sync
replicas to satisfy our requirements,

166
00:11:23,015 --> 00:11:24,653
and that's the expected behavior.

167
00:11:25,672 --> 00:11:30,843
Once we remove the network isolation,
the firewall rules, and the connectivity

168
00:11:30,848 --> 00:11:35,245
between the regions are restored,
we should see that the replicas are

169
00:11:35,252 --> 00:11:39,683
catching up and the cluster should
return to normal operations in some time

170
00:11:40,683 --> 00:11:44,788
The next test is to make sure
producer acks behave as expected.

171
00:11:45,773 --> 00:11:49,620
We start with all six replicas
available, and the producer should

172
00:11:49,629 --> 00:11:51,759
be able to write without any issues.

173
00:11:52,107 --> 00:11:53,096
This is a no-brainer.

174
00:11:53,804 --> 00:11:58,237
When we bring one region down, we
are left with four in-sync replicas,

175
00:11:58,946 --> 00:12:03,402
which is still above our configured
minimum value of three, so writes

176
00:12:03,499 --> 00:12:05,249
should continue to succeed still.

177
00:12:06,663 --> 00:12:11,153
If we bring down one more broker,
we still have three min in-sync

178
00:12:11,153 --> 00:12:16,079
replicas, which is three in-sync
replicas, which is still equal to

179
00:12:16,079 --> 00:12:17,975
the minimum number that is required.

180
00:12:18,138 --> 00:12:19,644
So all good so far.

181
00:12:20,943 --> 00:12:25,243
But the moment we bring down
a fourth broker, we are left

182
00:12:25,244 --> 00:12:27,085
with only two in-sync replicas.

183
00:12:27,645 --> 00:12:33,149
Since that falls below our configured
min in-sync replicas of three, producers

184
00:12:33,205 --> 00:12:35,164
should start receiving write errors.

185
00:12:35,819 --> 00:12:37,090
No write should succeed.

186
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:39,920
They won't receive the
ack back from the server.

187
00:12:40,768 --> 00:12:44,548
This is an important test to validate
that our clients are configured

188
00:12:44,549 --> 00:12:49,605
correctly and that Kafka continues
accepting writes when it's safe to do

189
00:12:49,611 --> 00:12:53,983
so, but most importantly, it rejects
them when the minimum durability

190
00:12:53,988 --> 00:12:56,471
requirements can no longer be met.

191
00:12:57,481 --> 00:13:02,189
This test also involves working with
your clients because we are going to

192
00:13:02,193 --> 00:13:04,484
be breaking them and inducing errors.

193
00:13:05,484 --> 00:13:08,099
The next test is to validate
the controller quorum.

194
00:13:08,516 --> 00:13:12,859
Let's focus specifically on the
dynamic controller quorum setup.

195
00:13:13,763 --> 00:13:17,421
We start by bringing down
one region, leaving us with

196
00:13:17,498 --> 00:13:19,009
three available controllers.

197
00:13:19,629 --> 00:13:23,838
We can then manually remove two
unavailable voters from the quorum

198
00:13:23,841 --> 00:13:28,460
configuration, bringing down the
total voters from five to three.

199
00:13:29,256 --> 00:13:34,345
At this point, we only need a majority
of two controllers to maintain quorum.

200
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:39,520
Now, we can try lo- rolling the
surviving controllers one at a time.

201
00:13:40,573 --> 00:13:45,912
Since we still maintain a majority during
each restart, the controller quorum should

202
00:13:45,912 --> 00:13:51,668
maintain working should and be re-- and
be available throughout the process.

203
00:13:52,522 --> 00:13:56,610
This test helps us validate that
the controller quorum can tolerate a

204
00:13:56,613 --> 00:14:01,261
regional failure while still allowing
us to perform maintenance if needed.

205
00:14:01,695 --> 00:14:06,279
So we have tested the brokers,
we have tested the controllers.

206
00:14:06,971 --> 00:14:13,243
Now let's test the WAN So far, we
have been testing complete regional

207
00:14:13,251 --> 00:14:15,853
failovers or complete network isolation.

208
00:14:16,624 --> 00:14:20,858
Now let's look at what happens
when the WAN is degraded rather

209
00:14:20,861 --> 00:14:22,536
than completely unavailable.

210
00:14:23,536 --> 00:14:27,935
When everything is healthy, client
latency stays within our expected SLOs.

211
00:14:28,541 --> 00:14:32,197
We can start by deliberately
introducing latencies between

212
00:14:32,222 --> 00:14:37,199
regions, gradually increasing it to
simules- simulate network degradation.

213
00:14:37,907 --> 00:14:41,794
As we do this, we should start
seeing an increase in client latency.

214
00:14:42,877 --> 00:14:46,382
Next, we can introduce
packet loss between regions.

215
00:14:47,074 --> 00:14:51,829
As packet loss increases, client
latency can raise significantly.

216
00:14:52,749 --> 00:14:57,215
If the degradation becomes severe
enough, replicas may start falling

217
00:14:57,221 --> 00:15:03,424
out of the ISR, and once the ISR drops
below the configured minimum ISR value,

218
00:15:03,824 --> 00:15:05,970
Kafka will stop accepting writes.

219
00:15:06,841 --> 00:15:11,565
How far we push this depends on how
aggressively we want to test the system.

220
00:15:12,733 --> 00:15:14,976
Now, there's an interesting scenario.

221
00:15:15,569 --> 00:15:20,230
While the WAN is degraded and the
clients are expecting, the clients

222
00:15:20,230 --> 00:15:25,301
are experiencing high latency, what
happens if we just completely cut the

223
00:15:25,302 --> 00:15:27,075
connection to the affected region?

224
00:15:28,333 --> 00:15:33,841
This will trigger read-- leader
reelections, but once it, it stabilizes,

225
00:15:34,308 --> 00:15:38,360
you would be surprised to see that the
client latencies would go back to normal.

226
00:15:39,258 --> 00:15:43,435
The biggest takeaway for me
was that degraded WAN can be

227
00:15:43,445 --> 00:15:47,167
much, much more dangerous than
a completely unavailable one.

228
00:15:48,456 --> 00:15:52,799
This is why having a disaster
recovery policy for w- WAN

229
00:15:52,802 --> 00:15:54,614
degradation is very important.

230
00:15:55,479 --> 00:16:01,023
If the cluster can safely tolerate
losing a region, deliberately isolating

231
00:16:01,025 --> 00:16:05,789
it may be better than continued,
continuing to operate with severely

232
00:16:05,806 --> 00:16:08,119
degraded connectivity between regions.

233
00:16:08,819 --> 00:16:12,489
So that's one of the things that
we came up with first as a part

234
00:16:12,498 --> 00:16:14,399
of our disaster recovery policy.

235
00:16:15,399 --> 00:16:18,670
So far, we have looked at
different failure scenarios where

236
00:16:18,670 --> 00:16:22,521
we deliberately brought brokers
down, isolated the regions, and

237
00:16:22,522 --> 00:16:24,217
degraded the network connections.

238
00:16:24,935 --> 00:16:28,449
Now, let's look at what happens
when things come back online.

239
00:16:29,172 --> 00:16:33,755
Here we are comparing two recovery
scenarios after a regional outage.

240
00:16:34,399 --> 00:16:40,492
In the first scenario, recovery, is not
throttled, whereas in the second, we apply

241
00:16:40,561 --> 00:16:42,457
throttling to the replication traffic.

242
00:16:43,176 --> 00:16:47,912
As you can see, without throttling,
when the returning replicas have a

243
00:16:47,913 --> 00:16:53,760
significant amount of data to catch up
on, the surviving regions can experience

244
00:16:53,826 --> 00:16:59,672
additional resource pressure in terms
of CPU, disk IO, and network egress.

245
00:17:00,754 --> 00:17:05,090
This can severely impact client
latency, especially when there's

246
00:17:05,108 --> 00:17:07,163
a large replication backlog.

247
00:17:08,150 --> 00:17:12,480
With appropriate throttling, we
can control how aggressively these

248
00:17:12,501 --> 00:17:17,250
replicas catch up and reduce the
impact on live client traffic.

249
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:21,249
The trade-off here is longer
recovery of the region.

250
00:17:22,423 --> 00:17:26,809
The key takeaway is that planning
for failure alone is not enough.

251
00:17:26,892 --> 00:17:29,724
We should also deliberately
plan for recovery.

252
00:17:30,686 --> 00:17:36,476
The goal is to bring the failed regions
back online without significantly

253
00:17:36,492 --> 00:17:40,541
impacting the traffic that's
already being served to the clients.

254
00:17:41,541 --> 00:17:44,636
We have now walked through
six different tests.

255
00:17:45,346 --> 00:17:50,685
Each test validates a different aspect of
availability and durability guarantees.

256
00:17:51,706 --> 00:17:55,032
It's not enough to run
these tests just once.

257
00:17:56,015 --> 00:18:00,088
We need to keep testing, learning
from the results, and fine-tuning

258
00:18:00,088 --> 00:18:03,652
our configurations until we
achieve the desired behavior.

259
00:18:04,419 --> 00:18:06,386
Even then, testing shouldn't stop.

260
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:10,250
We should repeat these tests
whenever we make significant

261
00:18:10,250 --> 00:18:15,155
changes to the cluster architecture,
configuration, infrastructure, or if

262
00:18:15,158 --> 00:18:17,369
we see new client traffic patterns.

263
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:24,128
We even do DR routines once every
few months just to make sure that

264
00:18:24,153 --> 00:18:25,813
everything works as expected.

265
00:18:26,837 --> 00:18:31,216
Ultimately, running Kafka
across multiple regions doesn't

266
00:18:31,273 --> 00:18:33,232
automatically make it tier zero.

267
00:18:33,993 --> 00:18:38,641
What matters is understanding its failure
boundaries and proving that the system

268
00:18:38,646 --> 00:18:41,328
behaves as expected when things go wrong.

269
00:18:42,328 --> 00:18:44,619
I'll leave you all with one final thought.

270
00:18:45,581 --> 00:18:49,682
When it comes to mission-critical
infrastructure, we can't just assume

271
00:18:49,683 --> 00:18:54,141
that a system will survive a failure
because we have designed it to do so.

272
00:18:54,868 --> 00:18:59,962
We need to deliberately test those
failures, understand how the system

273
00:18:59,967 --> 00:19:04,217
behaves, and validate that our
availability and durability guarantees

274
00:19:04,348 --> 00:19:07,010
actually hold when it happens.

275
00:19:08,135 --> 00:19:09,924
Don't just hope it survives.

276
00:19:10,025 --> 00:19:11,627
Break it and know it.

277
00:19:12,048 --> 00:19:12,472
Thank you.
